# 8. Facts the code rests on, each checked
- git push --porcelain prints "<flag>\t<src>:<dst>\t<summary>"; --force-with-lease=<ref>:<sha> refuses a stale ref and
  --force-with-lease=<ref>: refuses an existing one (exit 1, "[rejected] (stale info)"), checked on scratch repositories.
- git ls-remote <url> 'refs/heads/kiro/*' matches branches with deeper names (kiro/a/b/c), checked.
- systemd's %d specifier is the credentials directory (accepted by systemd-analyze verify; %Q is refused).
- GitLab: a personal access token over Git-over-HTTPS takes any non-blank user name
  (https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/user/profile/personal_access_tokens.html).
- GitHub fine-grained tokens: POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{n}/comments and PATCH /repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{n} are
  covered by "Pull requests" write; statuses by "Commit statuses"
  (https://docs.github.com/en/rest/authentication/permissions-required-for-fine-grained-personal-access-tokens).
- GitHub secondary rate limits: about 80 content-generating requests a minute and 500 an hour; wait for Retry-After or at
  least a minute (https://docs.github.com/en/rest/using-the-rest-api/rate-limits-for-the-rest-api).
- systemd credential files may carry ACLs instead of mode 0400 (systemd issue 29435), so the bridge does not check a token
  file's mode; the runbook keeps the files root-only, mode 0600.
